"The surah begins with an oath by Mount Tur. Tur means 'mountain' in Syriac and is Mount Sinai, where Moses (Musa) heard the word of Allah. It also symbolizes the flight from matter to meaning. Next, an oath is sworn by an inscribed book written on outspread parchment. This book is neatly written on fine parchment called 'raqq'. Some have considered it to be the Torah, some the record of deeds, and others the Preserved Tablet (al-Lawh al-Mahfuz) or the Quran. The soundest view, based on verse 13 of Surah al-Isra, is that it is the record of deeds. Furthermore, an oath is sworn by the Inhabited House (al-Bayt al-Ma'mur)—that is, a house in the heavens. This house is visited every day by seventy thousand angels. According to Hasan al-Basri, however, this is the Kaaba. Al-Baydawi says it is the heart of the believer. An oath is sworn by the elevated ceiling—that is, the sky or the Throne—and by the swelling sea (al-Bahr al-Masjur). Masjur means inflamed, filled, mixed, or empty. It is also said that this sea is the one in which Pharaoh drowned.\n\nThe answer to these oaths is that the punishment of your Lord will inevitably take place. On that Day, the heaven will sway violently, and the mountains will move from their places and turn into particles of dust. On that Day, woe to those who deny the Hereafter! They amuse themselves in a mire they have plunged into, engaging in vain pursuits instead of believing and preparing. On that Day, they will be thrust violently into Hell, and it will be said to them, 'This is the Fire that you used to deny.' They had called the revelation that foretold this Fire magic; now they are asked, 'Is this magic, then, which has actually come to pass?'\n\nThe righteous (muttaqin), on the other hand, will be in gardens and bliss. Those who believe, and whose offspring follow them in faith, are raised to the rank of their forefathers in Paradise along with their offspring. This occurs by virtue of the offspring's own faith, not merely through the earning of their fathers. Allah will not deprive them of anything from their deeds. Every person is bound to what they have earned. The righteous will be abundantly provided with fruits and meat of their desiring. There they will pass a cup to one another, wherein there is no vain talk nor inducement to sin. Circling around them will be youthful servants, as if they were hidden pearls. Turning to one another, they will say: 'Indeed, we used to be in fear earlier among our families. But Allah was gracious to us and protected us from the scorching punishment (samum). Indeed, we used to supplicate Him. Truly, He is the Beneficent, the Merciful.'\n\nO Muhammad! Remind and give counsel. By the grace of your Lord, you are neither a soothsayer nor a madman. The polytheists say, 'He is a poet; we await for him the calamities of time.' Say: 'Wait, and I too am waiting with you.' Is it their intellect that commands them to do this? Or are they a transgressing people? Or do they say, 'He invented it'? Nay, they do not believe. Let them produce a discourse like it, if they are truthful! Or were they created without a creator? Or did they create the heavens and the earth? Nay, they do not reflect and comprehend the truth.\n\nOr are the treasures of your Lord with them? Or are they the controllers of everything? Or do they have a ladder by which they eavesdrop? Then let their eavesdropper bring a clear proof! Is it that daughters belong to Him and sons to you? Or are you asking them for a fee, so that they are weighed down by a burden of debt? Or is the unseen with them, and they write it down? Or do they intend to set a trap? It is those who disbelieve who are trapped! Or do they have a deity other than Allah? Exalted is Allah far above whatever they associate with Him!\n\nEven if they were to see a fragment falling from the sky, they would say, 'It is just clouds stacked in heaps.' So leave them until they meet their Day in which they will be struck down. On that Day, none of their ploys will avail them, nor will they be helped. And surely, for those who do wrong, there is a punishment besides this, but most of them do not know. So be patient for the judgment of your Lord, for indeed you are before Our eyes. And glorify your Lord with praise when you arise. And glorify Him during a portion of the night and at the setting of the stars.\n\nThe phrase 'when you arise' mentioned in verse 49 commands glorifying and praising Allah when rising from a gathering. In a hadith narrated by Abu Dawud and al-Nasa'i, whenever the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) was about to rise from a gathering, he would supplicate: 'O Allah, I declare Your transcendence above every imperfection and praise You. I bear witness that there is no god but You, I ask Your forgiveness, and I turn to You in repentance,' and he would state that this prayer serves as an expiation for those present in the gathering.\n\nSome scholars have stated that what is meant by 'when you arise' in this verse is the time when you get out of bed and stand for prayer. According to this view, the verse points to a supplication to be recited upon standing for prayer: 'O Allah, I declare Your transcendence above all imperfections and praise You alone. How exalted is Your name, and how sublime is Your majesty! There is no god but You.'\n\nThe subsequent expression 'and glorify Him during the night' commands worshipping during a portion of the night. 'At the setting of the stars' points to the end of the night, the pre-dawn time (sahar). Scholars among the Companions and Successors, such as Umar, Ali, Abu Hurairah, and Hasan al-Basri (may Allah be pleased with them), stated that what is meant by night glorification is supererogatory worship, while what is meant by glorification at the setting of the stars is the two-rak'ah sunnah prayer of the dawn prayer (fajr).\n\nSurah at-Tur concludes by pointing to the arrival of morning with the phrase 'the setting of the stars,' and there is a beautiful correlation between this ending and Surah an-Najm beginning with the verse, 'By the star when it descends.' This link between the two surahs demonstrates the mutual harmony of the verses of the Quran.\n\nThese verses emphasize that a believer must remember Allah at every moment of life. Glorifying Him upon rising, while worshipping at night, and during the pre-dawn hours is a requirement of faith. Moreover, these glorifications serve as expiation for sins and bring a person closer to Allah."